From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Alejandro G. Castro" <alex@igalia.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 16:42:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] [Android] Instrument the main-thread waits and the
 composite that stall a scroll

Touch-driven scrolling is asynchronous since the coordinated touch-event work, but a
sequence can still be classified Synchronous, a wheel event can still need blocking DOM
dispatch, and the scrolling thread still blocks on the main thread's rendering update once
a frame. A scroll can also stall with no main-thread involvement at all, when the composite
itself overruns the frame. None of that was observable on Android: the port builds with
-DUSE_SYSPROF_CAPTURE=OFF, so every WTF::tracePoint() and every signpost compiled to
nothing.

Add an ATrace backend for both, so the instrumentation WebKit already has lands in the same
systrace/Perfetto timeline as SurfaceFlinger, input dispatch and the scheduler. Tracepoint
slice names are derived from the TracePointCode enumerators rather than a hand-written
table, so codes added upstream show up without further work. Signposts are keyed by a
pointer and may begin and end on different threads, so they map to process-scoped sections
rather than the thread-scoped kind, and HAVE(SIGNPOSTS) now says when the helpers that build
their descriptions are worth compiling.

Annotate the points that decide whether the main thread is on the critical path, naming the
reason in the slice: the tracking type each touch event gets and which non-passive listener
forced it, the wheel processing steps, any scrolling node with synchronous scrolling
reasons, the touch round trip and how many moves coalesced behind it, and how long each wait
for the main thread actually took.

Then split the composite, which the signposts only bounded as a whole: the damage-collect
walk, the draw walk, and the GPU handover, with flush() separated from submit() because they
fail differently - flush() turns recorded draws into GPU commands, where a program compile or
a texture upload lands, while submit() only hands the command buffer over, where driver queue
back-pressure would land. Each intermediate surface a layer asks for is named and counted, and
so is the GPU resource growth a frame causes.

Two levels are opt-in because of what they cost: debug.wpe.atrace=layers adds a slice per
layer painted, and =shaders reports every GPU program Skia compiles, which needs a
PersistentCache whose load() always misses - Skia then fetches the compiled binary to hand
over, which on Mali roughly triples the worst composite. The timing of those compilations is
the signal, not their cost.

Rebased onto the backend from https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/78343: the backend
additions here are behind its USE(ATRACE_CAPTURE) option instead of OS(ANDROID).
Style checker fixes: include order, switch indent, std::string_view instead of strcmp(),
lambda spacing.
